{
 "<1>{0}</1>Notice to the other side": "/knowledge/blog/adjudix-process-flow-chart",
 "The Secretariat notifies the respondent, who files a response and any counter-claim.": "/knowledge/blog/adjudix-process-flow-chart",
 "<1>{0}</1>Appointment of the neutral": "/knowledge/blog/adjudix-process-flow-chart",
 "A mediator, conciliator, or arbitrator is appointed from the Professional Panel — by the parties, by preference ranking, or by draw of lots — after written disclosure of independence.": "/knowledge/blog/adjudix-process-flow-chart",
 "<1>{0}</1>Mediation phase (Med-Arb and mediation)": "/knowledge/blog/adjudix-process-flow-chart",
 "Joint and private sessions within an agreed window to find terms both parties can accept.": "/knowledge/blog/adjudix-process-flow-chart",
 "Settled": "/knowledge/blog/adjudix-process-flow-chart",
 "Terms are recorded as a settlement agreement or a consent award, binding and enforceable.": "/knowledge/blog/adjudix-process-flow-chart",
 "Not settled": "/knowledge/blog/adjudix-process-flow-chart",
 "In Med-Arb the case converts to arbitration, with the same neutral (Option A) or a separate one (Option B).": "/knowledge/blog/adjudix-process-flow-chart",
 "<1>{0}</1>Arbitration": "/knowledge/blog/adjudix-process-flow-chart",
 "Pleadings, case management, evidence, and hearings — online, offline, or hybrid — under the Adjudix Code of ADR Procedure.": "/knowledge/blog/adjudix-process-flow-chart",
 "<1>{0}</1>Award": "/knowledge/blog/adjudix-process-flow-chart",
 "A written, reasoned, signed award that decides the dispute and the costs.": "/knowledge/blog/adjudix-process-flow-chart",
 "<1>{0}</1>Implementation": "/knowledge/blog/adjudix-process-flow-chart",
 "Adjudix supports implementing the award or settlement, in India and abroad.": "/knowledge/blog/adjudix-process-flow-chart",
 "Where urgent relief fits": "/knowledge/blog/adjudix-process-flow-chart",
 "If a party needs protection before the tribunal exists — freezing an asset, stopping a sale — it can apply for emergency arbitration at any point before the tribunal is constituted. The emergency arbitrator's order holds the position until the tribunal takes over.": "/knowledge/blog/adjudix-process-flow-chart",
 "Flow chart": "/knowledge/blog/adjudix-process-flow-chart",
 "The Sections of the Arbitration Act That Matter Most — and How They Work in Practice | Adjudix Dispute Resolution Institute": "/knowledge/blog/arbitration-act-key-sections",
 "Arbitration · {0} min read": "/knowledge/blog/arbitration-act-key-sections",
 "The Arbitration and Conciliation Act, {0} has more than eighty sections, but a handful of them decide the course of almost every case. Knowing what they say — and how they play out — helps a party make better decisions from the first day of a dispute.": "/knowledge/blog/arbitration-act-key-sections",
 "Section {0} — the arbitration agreement": "/knowledge/blog/arbitration-act-key-sections",
 "Everything begins here. The agreement must be in writing, but writing is read broadly: a signed clause, an exchange of letters, emails, or other electronic communication, or pleadings in which one side asserts and the other does not deny it. In practice, the most common problem is not the absence of an agreement but a badly drafted one — an unclear seat, an unworkable appointment mechanism, or a clause that mixes arbitration with courts. That is why clause drafting deserves attention before a dispute arises.": "/knowledge/blog/arbitration-act-key-sections",
 "Section {0} — reference by a court": "/knowledge/blog/arbitration-act-key-sections",
 "If one party files a suit despite an arbitration agreement, the other can apply to the court to refer the matter to arbitration. The application must be made no later than the first statement on the substance of the dispute. Miss that window and the right to arbitrate may be lost — a practical reason to involve advisers early.": "/knowledge/blog/arbitration-act-key-sections",
 "Sections {0} and {1} — interim protection": "/knowledge/blog/arbitration-act-key-sections",
 "Section {0} lets a party approach a court for interim measures — preserving assets, securing the amount in dispute, appointing a receiver, or an injunction — before, during, or after the arbitration. Section {1} gives the arbitral tribunal the same range of powers, and its orders are enforceable as if they were orders of the court. Once a tribunal is in place, courts generally expect parties to seek interim relief from the tribunal first. Before the tribunal exists, an emergency arbitrator can fill the gap.": "/knowledge/blog/arbitration-act-key-sections",
 "Sections {0} and {1} — appointment and independence": "/knowledge/blog/arbitration-act-key-sections",
 "Section {0} governs how arbitrators are appointed and allows a court to step in when the agreed mechanism fails. Section {1} requires every prospective arbitrator to disclose, in writing, any circumstance likely to give rise to justifiable doubts about their independence or impartiality. Certain relationships listed in the Seventh Schedule make a person ineligible altogether, unless the parties expressly waive this in writing after the dispute has arisen. An institution with a vetted panel and a disclosure protocol takes most of this risk out of the process.": "/knowledge/blog/arbitration-act-key-sections",
 "Section {0} — the tribunal decides its own jurisdiction": "/knowledge/blog/arbitration-act-key-sections",
 "The tribunal can rule on objections to its own jurisdiction, including objections about the existence or validity of the arbitration agreement. The arbitration clause is treated as independent of the main contract, so a claim that the contract is void does not automatically defeat the clause. Jurisdictional objections must be raised no later than the statement of defence.": "/knowledge/blog/arbitration-act-key-sections",
 "Sections {0}A and {1}B — time limits and fast track": "/knowledge/blog/arbitration-act-key-sections",
 "For domestic arbitrations, the award must be made within twelve months of the completion of pleadings, extendable by six months by consent; any further extension requires the court. Section {0}B offers a fast-track procedure, decided largely on written submissions, with the award due within six months. These limits are a real advantage over ordinary litigation — and they reward parties who come prepared.": "/knowledge/blog/arbitration-act-key-sections",
 "Section {0} — settlement within arbitration": "/knowledge/blog/arbitration-act-key-sections",
 "A tribunal may encourage settlement and, with the parties' agreement, use mediation, conciliation, or other procedures at any time. A settlement reached this way can be recorded as an arbitral award on agreed terms, with the same status and effect as any other award. Section {0} is the statutory footing for hybrid processes such as Med-Arb.": "/knowledge/blog/arbitration-act-key-sections",
 "Sections {0} and {1} — challenge and enforcement": "/knowledge/blog/arbitration-act-key-sections",
 "An award can be challenged under Section {0} only on limited grounds — incapacity, an invalid agreement, lack of proper notice, matters beyond the reference, an improperly constituted tribunal, conflict with the public policy of India, and, for domestic awards, patent illegality on the face of the award. The application must be made within three months of receiving the award, extendable by thirty days for sufficient cause.": "/knowledge/blog/arbitration-act-key-sections",
 "Once that period has expired, or a challenge has been refused, Section {0} makes the award enforceable as if it were a decree of a court. Importantly, filing a challenge does not automatically stay enforcement; the court must grant a stay, usually on conditions such as a deposit of the amount awarded.": "/knowledge/blog/arbitration-act-key-sections",
 "Part II — foreign awards": "/knowledge/blog/arbitration-act-key-sections",
 "Awards made in countries that India recognises under the New York Convention can be enforced in India under Part II of the Act, subject to narrow refusal grounds. The same Convention lets an Indian-seated award be enforced abroad — the foundation of global implementation.": "/knowledge/blog/arbitration-act-key-sections",
 "The Basics of Arbitration: How It Works and When to Choose It | Adjudix Dispute Resolution Institute": "/knowledge/blog/basics-of-arbitration",
 "Arbitration is a private process in which the parties to a dispute agree to have it decided by one or more neutral persons — the arbitrators — instead of a court. The decision, called an award, is final and binding, and the law treats it with much the same force as a court decree. In India, arbitration is governed by the Arbitration and Conciliation Act, {0}, which is modelled on the UNCITRAL Model Law used across the world.": "/knowledge/blog/basics-of-arbitration",
 "Unlike mediation or conciliation, where a neutral helps the parties reach their own settlement, an arbitrator decides. That is what makes arbitration the natural choice when the parties need a definite outcome rather than a negotiated one.": "/knowledge/blog/basics-of-arbitration",
 "It starts with an agreement": "/knowledge/blog/basics-of-arbitration",
 "Arbitration is consensual. There must be an arbitration agreement in writing — usually a clause in the main contract, but it can also be a separate agreement signed after a dispute has arisen, or even an exchange of letters or emails that records the parties' consent. Once a valid agreement exists, a court before which a suit is filed on the same matter must, on a timely application, refer the parties to arbitration.": "/knowledge/blog/basics-of-arbitration",
 "How a case moves": "/knowledge/blog/basics-of-arbitration",
 "A typical institutional arbitration at Adjudix runs through these stages:": "/knowledge/blog/basics-of-arbitration",
 "Notice of arbitration — the claimant notifies the other side and files the request with the Secretariat.": "/knowledge/blog/basics-of-arbitration",
 "Constitution of the tribunal — a sole arbitrator or a three-member tribunal is appointed from the Adjudix Professional Panel, after each arbitrator discloses any circumstance that could affect their independence.": "/knowledge/blog/basics-of-arbitration",
 "Pleadings — the statement of claim, the statement of defence, and any counter-claim, with supporting documents.": "/knowledge/blog/basics-of-arbitration",
 "Case management — the tribunal fixes a timetable, decides how evidence will be taken, and settles whether hearings are online, offline, or both.": "/knowledge/blog/basics-of-arbitration",
 "Hearings and arguments — witnesses and experts are examined where needed; many document-heavy cases are decided largely on the papers.": "/knowledge/blog/basics-of-arbitration",
 "The award — a written, reasoned, signed decision, which the parties may then implement or, if necessary, enforce.": "/knowledge/blog/basics-of-arbitration",
 "Why parties choose arbitration": "/knowledge/blog/basics-of-arbitration",
 "Speed and predictability come first. The Act requires a domestic award to be made within twelve months of the completion of pleadings, extendable by six months by consent, and a fast-track procedure allows an award within six months on written submissions.": "/knowledge/blog/basics-of-arbitration",
 "Expertise is the second reason. The parties can have a construction dispute heard by an engineer, an accounts dispute by a chartered accountant, and a shareholder dispute by a corporate lawyer. Confidentiality is the third: proceedings are private, and the award need not become public. Finally, an award is easier to carry across borders than a court judgment, because India and more than {0} other countries are party to the New York Convention on the recognition and enforcement of foreign arbitral awards.": "/knowledge/blog/basics-of-arbitration",
 "When arbitration may not be right": "/knowledge/blog/basics-of-arbitration",
 "Some matters cannot be arbitrated — criminal offences, matrimonial status, guardianship, insolvency itself, and certain rights that the law reserves for courts or tribunals. And where the parties' relationship matters more than a ruling, mediation or a hybrid process may serve them better. A short consultation at the outset usually makes the right path clear.": "/knowledge/blog/basics-of-arbitration",
 "The Adjudix approach": "/knowledge/blog/basics-of-arbitration",
 "Every Adjudix arbitration is administered under the Adjudix Code of ADR Procedure, {0}, with a published fee schedule, a panel organised by profession, and a choice of online, offline, or hybrid hearings. And because an award is only worth what it recovers, Adjudix also supports the implementation of awards — in India and globally.": "/knowledge/blog/basics-of-arbitration",
 "Corporate ADR: Resolving Business Disputes Without Breaking the Business | Adjudix Dispute Resolution Institute": "/knowledge/blog/corporate-adr",
 "Corporate ADR · {0} min read": "/knowledge/blog/corporate-adr",
 "For a business, the cost of a dispute is rarely just the legal fees. It is management time, a stalled project, a frozen supply line, or a partnership that turns hostile. Corporate ADR is about resolving these disputes quickly and privately, in a way the business can plan for — rather than reacting when a dispute has already escalated.": "/knowledge/blog/corporate-adr",
 "Where corporate ADR applies": "/knowledge/blog/corporate-adr",
 "Shareholder, board, and promoter disputes, including deadlocks in closely held and family companies.": "/knowledge/blog/corporate-adr",
 "Vendor, supplier, distributor, and customer disputes under commercial contracts.": "/knowledge/blog/corporate-adr",
 "Partnership and LLP disputes between partners.": "/knowledge/blog/corporate-adr",
 "Technology, licensing, and intellectual property disputes.": "/knowledge/blog/corporate-adr",
 "High-value financial and M&A disputes, including earn-outs and warranty claims.": "/knowledge/blog/corporate-adr",
 "Cross-border commercial disputes with parties in several jurisdictions.": "/knowledge/blog/corporate-adr",
 "A standing arrangement, not an emergency call": "/knowledge/blog/corporate-adr",
 "The most effective corporate ADR is set up before it is needed. Adjudix works with companies to put consistent dispute resolution clauses across their contracts, agree escalation steps — negotiation, then mediation, then arbitration — and give in-house teams a single point of contact when something goes wrong. When a dispute arises, the process is already agreed, the panel is already available, and the timetable is already known.": "/knowledge/blog/corporate-adr",
 "Choosing the right mechanism": "/knowledge/blog/corporate-adr",
 "Not every corporate dispute needs an award. A dispute with a long-standing distributor may be best mediated; a deadlock between shareholders may suit a hybrid process; a claim against a defaulting customer may need arbitration and swift implementation. Commercial suits in India also generally require pre-institution mediation under Section {0}A of the Commercial Courts Act, {1}, unless urgent interim relief is sought — another reason to have a mediation route ready.": "/knowledge/blog/corporate-adr",
 "Confidentiality by design": "/knowledge/blog/corporate-adr",
 "Corporate disputes often involve price-sensitive information, trade secrets, or matters that a business would rather not see reported. ADR proceedings at Adjudix are confidential, hearings are private, and documents are handled through a secure online platform. Our corporate partnerships are themselves kept confidential.": "/knowledge/blog/corporate-adr",
 "How a corporate engagement works": "/knowledge/blog/corporate-adr",
 "A typical engagement begins with a review of the company's key contracts and the disputes it has faced in recent years. From this, Adjudix recommends a dispute resolution framework: standard clauses for each category of contract, escalation steps with time limits, and the mechanism best suited to each type of dispute. Where the company wishes, these clauses are drafted or redrafted by the Adjudix Contract & Clause Drafting team.": "/knowledge/blog/corporate-adr",
 "When a dispute arises, the in-house team contacts the Secretariat, which proposes neutrals with the right professional background, confirms the fee under the published schedule, and opens the case on the ODR Platform. Management receives a clear timetable from the first case management conference, so the dispute can be budgeted and planned for like any other business process.": "/knowledge/blog/corporate-adr",
 "Cross-border disputes": "/knowledge/blog/corporate-adr",
 "When the counterparty is abroad, the questions multiply: which law governs the contract, where the arbitration should be seated, in what language it should run, and — most importantly — where an award will need to be enforced. Choosing an arbitration seated in a New York Convention country, under institutional rules, keeps the award portable. Adjudix plans for implementation from the outset, so the forum and the procedure are chosen with the eventual recovery in mind.": "/knowledge/blog/corporate-adr",
 "The business case": "/knowledge/blog/corporate-adr",
 "A predictable process, decided by professionals who understand the industry, within a known time and on a published fee schedule — that is what corporate ADR offers. For a business, it turns disputes from open-ended risks into managed events.": "/knowledge/blog/corporate-adr",
 "Drafting an Arbitration Clause That Works When It Matters | Adjudix Dispute Resolution Institute": "/knowledge/blog/drafting-arbitration-clauses",
 "Contract Drafting · {0} min read": "/knowledge/blog/drafting-arbitration-clauses",
 "Dispute resolution clauses are often the last thing negotiated and the first thing argued over. A well-drafted clause sends the dispute straight to the right forum. A poor one invites a preliminary fight — in court — over where, how, and by whom the dispute should be decided, before the merits are even reached.": "/knowledge/blog/drafting-arbitration-clauses",
 "The essentials": "/knowledge/blog/drafting-arbitration-clauses",
 "A clear reference to arbitration — “shall be finally resolved by arbitration”, not “may be referred”.": "/knowledge/blog/drafting-arbitration-clauses",
 "The rules and the institution — for example, the latest version of the Adjudix Code of ADR Procedure, administered by Adjudix.": "/knowledge/blog/drafting-arbitration-clauses",
 "The seat — the legal home of the arbitration, which decides which courts supervise it. Name a city, and say it is the seat.": "/knowledge/blog/drafting-arbitration-clauses",
 "The number of arbitrators — one for most disputes, three for complex or high-value ones.": "/knowledge/blog/drafting-arbitration-clauses",
 "The language of the proceedings.": "/knowledge/blog/drafting-arbitration-clauses",
 "The governing law of the contract, especially in cross-border agreements.": "/knowledge/blog/drafting-arbitration-clauses",
 "Common mistakes": "/knowledge/blog/drafting-arbitration-clauses",
 "Naming both courts and arbitration as forums for the same disputes.": "/knowledge/blog/drafting-arbitration-clauses",
 "Naming a “venue” without saying which place is the seat.": "/knowledge/blog/drafting-arbitration-clauses",
 "An appointment process that gives one party the sole right to appoint the arbitrator — which courts have held to be invalid.": "/knowledge/blog/drafting-arbitration-clauses",
 "Referring to an institution or rules that do not exist, or misnaming them.": "/knowledge/blog/drafting-arbitration-clauses",
 "Pre-arbitration steps with no time limit, which can be used to delay.": "/knowledge/blog/drafting-arbitration-clauses",
 "Clauses in related contracts that point to different forums.": "/knowledge/blog/drafting-arbitration-clauses",
 "Going further": "/knowledge/blog/drafting-arbitration-clauses",
 "Good drafting goes beyond the basic clause. A tiered clause can require negotiation and mediation before arbitration, with fixed time limits for each step. A hybrid clause can opt into Med-Arb. The clause can provide for emergency arbitration, fast-track procedure for smaller claims, online hearings, confidentiality obligations, and how costs will be allocated. For groups of related contracts, consistent clauses allow disputes to be consolidated.": "/knowledge/blog/drafting-arbitration-clauses",
 "Seat, venue, and governing law": "/knowledge/blog/drafting-arbitration-clauses",
 "Three different questions are often confused. The seat is the legal home of the arbitration and determines which courts have supervisory jurisdiction, including over any challenge to the award. The venue is simply where hearings physically take place, and it can change from hearing to hearing without affecting the seat. The governing law is the law that applies to the contract itself. A clause that says “venue: Mumbai” without naming a seat leaves room for argument; a clause that says “the seat of arbitration shall be Mumbai” does not.": "/knowledge/blog/drafting-arbitration-clauses",
 "In cross-border contracts, the law governing the arbitration agreement can also differ from the law governing the main contract. Stating it expressly avoids a preliminary dispute that can take months to resolve.": "/knowledge/blog/drafting-arbitration-clauses",
 "Review before signing": "/knowledge/blog/drafting-arbitration-clauses",
 "A short review of the dispute resolution clause before a contract is signed costs very little compared with the cost of litigating over a defective one. The checklist is simple: Is the reference to arbitration mandatory? Are the rules and institution correctly named? Is the seat clear? Is the appointment procedure fair to both sides? Do related contracts point to the same forum? If any answer is uncertain, the clause should be fixed now, while the parties are still on good terms.": "/knowledge/blog/drafting-arbitration-clauses",
 "A model to start from": "/knowledge/blog/drafting-arbitration-clauses",
 "Adjudix publishes model clauses for arbitration, mediation, and hybrid procedures, each referring disputes to the latest version of the Adjudix Code of ADR Procedure. They are a sound starting point for most contracts. For complex, high-value, or cross-border agreements, the Adjudix Contract & Clause Drafting service tailors the clause — and, where needed, the whole contract — to the transaction.": "/knowledge/blog/drafting-arbitration-clauses",
 "Emergency Arbitration: Urgent Relief Before the Tribunal Exists | Adjudix Dispute Resolution Institute": "/knowledge/blog/emergency-arbitration",
 "Emergency Arbitration · {0} min read": "/knowledge/blog/emergency-arbitration",
 "Constituting an arbitral tribunal takes time — notices must be served, arbitrators nominated, disclosures made. But some disputes cannot wait. A counterparty may be about to sell the only asset that could satisfy a claim, invoke a bank guarantee, or hand confidential information to a competitor. Emergency arbitration exists for exactly these moments.": "/knowledge/blog/emergency-arbitration",
 "What it is": "/knowledge/blog/emergency-arbitration",
 "Under institutional rules, a party can apply for urgent interim relief before the arbitral tribunal is constituted. The institution appoints an emergency arbitrator — usually within a day or two — who hears the parties on an expedited timetable and makes an order. That order binds the parties until the full tribunal is in place, when the tribunal may confirm, modify, or set it aside.": "/knowledge/blog/emergency-arbitration",
 "Is it enforceable in India?": "/knowledge/blog/emergency-arbitration",
 "The Arbitration and Conciliation Act, {0} does not use the words “emergency arbitrator”, but the Supreme Court has settled the position for arbitrations seated in India. In Amazon.com NV Investment Holdings LLC v. Future Retail Ltd. ({1}), the Court held that an emergency arbitrator's order under institutional rules the parties had agreed to is an order under Section {2}({3}) of the Act, enforceable under Section {4}({5}) as if it were an order of the court.": "/knowledge/blog/emergency-arbitration",
 "For arbitrations seated outside India, an emergency order is not directly enforceable as such, and parties typically approach an Indian court under Section {0} for equivalent relief. Choosing an India-seated, institution-administered arbitration therefore matters when urgent relief is likely.": "/knowledge/blog/emergency-arbitration",
 "The Adjudix procedure": "/knowledge/blog/emergency-arbitration",
 "Application — filed with the Secretariat, setting out the relief sought, the urgency, and the facts, with the emergency arbitrator fee deposited in full.": "/knowledge/blog/emergency-arbitration",
 "Appointment — an emergency arbitrator is appointed from the panel promptly after the application is registered, following written disclosure.": "/knowledge/blog/emergency-arbitration",
 "Hearing — the emergency arbitrator fixes a short schedule, usually online, and may hear both sides within days.": "/knowledge/blog/emergency-arbitration",
 "Order — a reasoned order granting or refusing relief, binding until the tribunal decides otherwise.": "/knowledge/blog/emergency-arbitration",
 "Hand-over — the matter continues before the regular tribunal, which reviews the emergency order.": "/knowledge/blog/emergency-arbitration",
 "Typical reliefs": "/knowledge/blog/emergency-arbitration",
 "Restraining the sale, transfer, or encumbrance of assets.": "/knowledge/blog/emergency-arbitration",
 "Preserving evidence, documents, or data.": "/knowledge/blog/emergency-arbitration",
 "Restraining the invocation of a bank guarantee, where the law permits.": "/knowledge/blog/emergency-arbitration",
 "Maintaining the status quo under a contract until the tribunal can decide.": "/knowledge/blog/emergency-arbitration",
 "What the applicant must show": "/knowledge/blog/emergency-arbitration",
 "Emergency relief is exceptional, and an emergency arbitrator will expect the applicant to make out its case clearly. In practice, that usually means showing a prima facie case on the merits, that the balance of convenience favours relief, and that the applicant would suffer harm that cannot adequately be compensated later if relief is refused. The applicant must also explain why the matter cannot wait for the tribunal to be constituted.": "/knowledge/blog/emergency-arbitration",
 "Full and frank disclosure is essential. Where an application is heard at very short notice, the applicant must put before the emergency arbitrator the facts that cut against it as well as those that support it. An order obtained by concealment is likely to be set aside, often with costs.": "/knowledge/blog/emergency-arbitration",
 "Emergency arbitration or a court?": "/knowledge/blog/emergency-arbitration",
 "A party can still approach a court under Section {0} for interim relief before the arbitration begins. The advantages of emergency arbitration are speed, confidentiality, and a decision-maker with subject-matter expertise. A court may be the better option where relief is needed against a third party who is not bound by the arbitration agreement, or where coercive measures only a court can impose are required. The two routes can complement each other, and the choice should be made on the facts.": "/knowledge/blog/emergency-arbitration",
 "Planning for it": "/knowledge/blog/emergency-arbitration",
 "Emergency arbitration is only available if the arbitration agreement or the chosen rules provide for it. A clause that refers disputes to arbitration under the Adjudix Code of ADR Procedure brings in the emergency procedure automatically — one more reason the clause deserves care when the contract is signed.": "/knowledge/blog/emergency-arbitration",
 "The Hybrid ADR Process (Med-Arb): Settlement First, Certainty Always | Adjudix Dispute Resolution Institute": "/knowledge/blog/hybrid-adr-med-arb",
 "Most disputes end in a settlement. The trouble is that parties often walk into a process that cannot produce one — or into a mediation that, if it fails, leaves them starting again in court. The hybrid model of dispute resolution solves both problems. It begins with mediation and, if settlement is not reached, converts into arbitration that ends in a binding award. Settlement is attempted first; a final outcome is assured either way.": "/knowledge/blog/hybrid-adr-med-arb"
}
